{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ad1e2aa5033dde5cb","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QXRrq61ytHCNXeWfzB2Uc5","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate experimental design, statistical rigor, completeness of ablations, reproducibility, and whether the evidence supports the claims.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Pairwise Type Confusability Predicts Schema-Induced Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper studies why training-free (in-context) LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a multi-channel degradation model with two orthogonal channels:\n- Channel 1 (Count → Recall): More types dilute attention, causing entity omission\n- Channel 2 (Confusability → Type Confusion): Semantically similar co-present types cause type substitution errors\n\nKey metric: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type label embeddings. Pairwise SCS correlates with empirical confusion rates (r ≥ 0.12, p < 10^-6) across NER (Few-NERD, 66 types), RE (DocRED, 96 relations), and ED (MAVEN, 168 event types), and across two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B).\n\nThe paper operationalizes this into SDGD (Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition):\n- Step 1: Build confusability graph from pairwise SCS (threshold τ=0.8)\n- Step 2: Graph coloring assigns types to batches separating confusable pairs\n- Step 3: Batched extraction with merge-dedup\n- Two variants: SDGD-zero (embedding-based, zero calibration) and SDGD-cal (empirically calibrated)\n\n## Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Section 3: Multi-Channel Degradation Model**\n- §3.1 Count curves: DocRED (96 rel) accuracy 78.2%→35.8% over k=1..96; LLaMA on Few-NERD recall 0.598→0.302\n- §3.2 BCS-controlled: DocRED at k=8: low-BCS 0.745 vs high-BCS 0.462 (Δ=-28.3pp); at k=16: 0.718 vs 0.431 (Δ=-28.7pp)\n- §3.3 2×2 factorial on Few-NERD: {count=6,22}×{low,high BCS}. ANOVA: count F=8.42 p=0.006 η²=0.18; BCS F=14.73 p=0.001 η²=0.32; interaction F=0.31 p=0.42 η²=0.01. R²=0.74. Error channel decomposition: count drives recall (Δ=-0.099), BCS drives confusion (3.5× increase)\n\n**Section 4: Pairwise Type Confusability**\n- §4.1 Pairwise SCS-confusion correlation: Few-NERD r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12), MAVEN r=0.120 (p≈0), DocRED r=0.131 (p=3.8e-9)\n- Cross-model: Qwen3 r=0.156 vs LLaMA r=0.138, confusion ratios 10-28× vs 8-19×, BCS Δ at k=12: -10.1pp vs -10.5pp\n- §4.2 Error taxonomy by BCS bin: intra-batch type substitution 0%→49.4% from BCS=0 to BCS≥0.95 (ρ=1.0, χ²=6952)\n- §4.3 Centrality confound: per-type mean BCS correlates positively with F1 on MAVEN (ρ=+0.189 Qwen3, +0.208 MiniLM) — opposite direction. Pairwise signal remains valid (MiniLM r=0.120)\n\n**Section 5: SDGD**\n- §5.2 Few-NERD: single-pass F1=0.445, random v2 0.458 (+1.3pp), SDGD v2 0.480 (+3.5pp). 63% from grouping, 37% from batch reduction. SDGD σ=0.008 vs random σ=0.015\n- Per-type v2 F1=0.492 at 6.3× latency; SDGD captures 97.6% quality at 15.9% cost\n- SDGD-cal F1=0.493 (+4.8pp over single-pass, +1.3pp over SDGD-zero). SCS-confusion ρ=0.42, τ=0.30\n- §5.3 MAVEN: single-pass 0.247, random 0.265, SDGD-zero 0.282, SDGD-cal 0.298, per-type 0.291. SDGD-cal > per-type\n\n**Section 6: Analysis**\n- §6.1 SCS proxy validation: best embedding ρ=0.38-0.43 per dataset. Qwen3 best for Few-NERD (cross-domain), MiniLM best for MAVEN (homogeneous)\n- §6.2 Cross-task scaling: BCS effect -9.1pp (16-rel) → -31.2pp (80-rel) → -28.5pp (96-rel), ~3.4× amplification\n\n**Section 7: Conclusion + Limitations**\n- Only decoder-only LLMs; moderate effect sizes (r=0.12-0.16); latency overhead 8-12×\n\n## Experimental Design Reference (from review context)\nThe paper was designed to include these experiments:\n1. exp-005: DocRED count curve + BCS-controlled (96 relations) — INCLUDED\n2. exp-006: 2×2 factorial (count × confusability) — INCLUDED\n3. exp-007: BCS-binned error taxonomy — INCLUDED\n4. exp-009: SDGD v2 on MAVEN (168 types) — INCLUDED\n5. exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD — INCLUDED\n6. exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN — INCLUDED\n7. exp-012: LLaMA cross-model validation — INCLUDED\n8. exp-013: Random batching ablation — INCLUDED\n9. exp-014: Per-type splitting ablation — INCLUDED\n10. exp-015: SCS proxy validation — INCLUDED\n\nAll 10 planned experiments are included.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Experimental Design Quality**: Is the factorial design sound? Are controls adequate? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the correlation analyses appropriate? Is the ANOVA valid? Effect sizes?\n3. **Ablation Completeness**: Are the SDGD ablations (random vs SDGD vs per-type) sufficient?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided? Hyperparameters, prompts, seeds?\n5. **Evidence-Claim Alignment**: Does the evidence actually support each of the 3 claims?\n6. **Weaknesses**: What are the methodological gaps?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Analyses\n[What would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"06ce85cb-a1e9-468e-8bb4-2626a8603740","session_id":"08464124-1424-4e01-848b-117232553f88"}
