{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017dytxZacLJvxYiWWs7RzFQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CAtQzZZJe5eyio9ijx2Fag","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"schema-dag-extraction","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7876,"cache_read_input_tokens":16894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7876,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":62,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01McSQGkja8PgipZqGC6MdqF","session_id":"08464124-1424-4e01-848b-117232553f88","uuid":"b9ecd4b7-7dc8-47a0-9d56-f380b36dd6dd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CAtQzZZJe5eyio9ijx2Fag","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\\\\n- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\\\\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\\\\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3 conditions) — Revised per D012\\\\n\\\\n1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. The signal is strongest and most robust at the **type-pair level** (pairwise SCS → confusion), validated across NER (Few-NERD), RE (FewRel), and ED (MAVEN with MiniLM). Per-type aggregation (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas. Evidence required: (a) significant pairwise SCS-confusion correlation on >=2 datasets, (b) extreme-batch accuracy gap on FewRel, (c) >=2 models.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\\\\n\\\\n3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the pairwise-similarity-thresholded graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). SDGD directly leverages pairwise confusability (validated signal), avoiding per-type aggregation confounds. Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine — Revised per D012\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). No prior work isolates confusability from count, or characterizes when aggregated confusability measures lose signal.\\\\n\\\\n- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE across three tasks (NER, RE, ED). We discover: (1) a multi-channel degradation model (count→recall, confusability→type confusion), (2) pairwise type confusability is a robust cross-task signal, (3) per-type aggregation of confusability is confounded by type centrality in homogeneous schemas. We operationalize pairwise confusability into SDGD, a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\\\\n\\\\n- **Three-task validation**:\\\\n  - NER (Few-NERD, 66 types, cross-domain): Pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), per-type ρ=-0.293 (p=0.02), 10-28x confusion ratio\\\\n  - RE (FewRel, 80 relations, cross-domain): BCS-controlled Δ=-31.2pp (80-rel formal); count curve acc 85.7%→50.4%; BCS effect scales 3.4x vs 16-rel\\\\n  - ED (MAVEN, 168 event types, same-domain): Pairwise MiniLM r=0.120 (p≈0); per-type positive (centrality confound case study)\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"Pairwise type confusability independently predicts extraction degradation across NER, RE, and ED tasks (r=0.12-0.16, p<1e-6), with 10-28x more type confusion errors in high-confusability batches. On semantically homogeneous schemas, per-type aggregation obscures the signal through centrality confounding, but pairwise confusability remains valid. SDGD-zero, operating on pairwise similarity, improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Core Insight — Multi-Channel Degradation + Pairwise Robustness\\\\n\\\\nSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\\\\n- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt → attention dilution → entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\\\\n- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types → interference during type assignment → correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\\\\n\\\\nThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable.\\\\n\\\\n**Pairwise vs Aggregated Confusability**:\\\\n- **Pairwise confusability** (SCS between type pairs) is robust across all tasks and schema types. Validated on Few-NERD (Qwen3, r=0.156) and MAVEN (MiniLM, r=0.120).\\\\n- **Per-type aggregation** (mean BCS = average pairwise similarity to all other types) introduces a centrality confound: in semantically homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), types with many semantic neighbors are central, well-represented types that LLMs extract well → positive correlation (high mean BCS → high F1), masking the pairwise confusability signal.\\\\n- **SDGD operates at pairwise level**, directly using the validated signal to construct the confusability graph for decomposition. It avoids the aggregation confound entirely.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence Hierarchy (D012)\\\\n\\\\n### Tier 1: PRIMARY — Pairwise SCS → Confusion\\\\n\\\\nThe cleanest evidence. Directly measures: are semantically similar type pairs more often confused?\\\\n\\\\n**Few-NERD (NER)** — Qwen3 embeddings:\\\\n- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs)\\\\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\\\\n- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\\\\n- Immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\\\\n\\\\n**MAVEN (ED)** — MiniLM embeddings:\\\\n- Pearson r=+0.120 (p≈0, n=11026 type pairs)\\\\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=+0.191 (p<0.001)\\\\n- Cross-task replication on same-domain schema\\\\n- Note: Qwen3 pairwise not significant on MAVEN (r=-0.007, p=0.41) — embedding choice matters for pairwise signal in homogeneous schemas\\\\n\\\\n### Tier 2: STRONG SECONDARY — FewRel Cross-Task Replication\\\\n\\\\nLarge effect size, cross-task (RE), scaling with schema complexity:\\\\n\\\\n**Formal 80-rel study** (exp_fewrel_formal_80rel):\\\\n- Count curve: acc 85.7% (count=1) → 50.4% (count=80), BCS 0→0.97\\\\n- BCS-controlled: count_8 low-BCS 82.3% vs high-BCS 51.0% (Δ=-31.2pp)\\\\n- BCS-controlled: count_16 low-BCS 82.8% vs high-BCS 51.6% (Δ=-31.2pp)\\\\n- **Scaling**: vs 16-rel, degradation 2.8x (35.3pp vs 12.6pp), BCS effect 3.4x (-31.2pp vs -9.1pp)\\\\n\\\\n**16-rel sanity check** (exp_fewrel_formal):\\\\n- count_8: low-BCS 88.9% vs high-BCS 79.8% (Δ=-9.1pp)\\\\n\\\\n**Pilot extreme batches** (fewrel_pilot, MiniLM):\\\\n- count_4: Δ=-20.9pp, count_8: Δ=-50.0pp, count_16: Δ=-48.6pp\\\\n\\\\nKey insight: confusability effect amplifies with schema size — direct evidence for multi-channel degradation scaling\\\\n\\\\n### Tier 3: SUPPORTING — Few-NERD Per-type Analysis\\\\n\\\\nBridges pairwise and batch-level, demonstrates per-type signal in cross-domain schema:\\\\n- Spearman ρ=-0.293 (p=0.020, n=63 types, freq≥5) — Qwen3 embeddings\\\\n- Partial r=-0.200 (p=0.116) controlling for log-frequency\\\\n- Quartile comparison: high-BCS types F1=0.228 vs low-BCS F1=0.310 (Δ=-8.2pp)\\\\n- BCS-frequency uncorrelated at type level (r=0.042, p=0.74)\\\\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ≈0 (p=0.97) — extraction model alignment required for per-type signal\\\\n\\\\n### Tier 4: DISCUSSION — MAVEN Per-type Centrality Confound\\\\n\\\\nMethodological case study on when aggregated confusability measures fail:\\\\n- Qwen3 per-type: ρ=+0.189 (p=0.021) — OPPOSITE direction\\\\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ=+0.208 (p=0.011) — ALSO positive, ruling out embedding compression as sole explanation\\\\n- Both embeddings agree: positive direction on MAVEN is an intrinsic property of the type structure\\\\n- Explanation: in homogeneous schemas, mean BCS conflates confusability with type centrality (types with many semantic neighbors → common/central types → higher F1)\\\\n- This is stronger than \\\\\\\"domain boundary\\\\\\\" — it identifies the specific mechanism (aggregation confound) rather than just the boundary condition\\\\n\\\\n### Tier 5: MECHANISTIC — Error Taxonomy\\\\n\\\\nConfusion rate differential demonstrates the mechanism:\\\\n- Extreme batches: high-BCS 10-28x more type confusion errors vs low-BCS\\\\n- Random batches: 1.7-2.4x ratio (still significant, smaller effect)\\\\n- Type confusion errors increase with count AND with confusability (additive)\\\\n\\\\n## Embedding Ablation (D010)\\\\n\\\\n2×3 matrix: {Qwen3, MiniLM} × {Few-NERD, FewRel, MAVEN}\\\\n\\\\n### Per-type (Layer 2)\\\\n| | Few-NERD | FewRel | MAVEN |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| Qwen3 | ρ=-0.293 ✓ | pending | ρ=+0.189 ✗ |\\\\n| MiniLM | ρ≈0 ✗ | Δ=21-50pp ✓ | ρ=+0.208 ✗ |\\\\n\\\\n### Pairwise (Layer 1)\\\\n| | Few-NERD | MAVEN |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Qwen3 | r=0.156 ✓ | r=-0.007 ✗ |\\\\n| MiniLM | — | r=+0.120 ✓ |\\\\n\\\\n**Interpretation**: No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings (Qwen3) capture per-type confusability in cross-domain schemas. Generic embeddings (MiniLM) capture pairwise confusability in same-domain schemas. SDGD should use task-appropriate embeddings; SDGD-cal (empirical confusion) bypasses embedding choice entirely.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit Risks\\\\n\\\\n- **\\\\\\\"Just an empirical study\\\\\\\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \\\\\\\"Lost in the Middle\\\\\\\" (TACL 2024).\\\\n- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios — clinical, regulatory, multilingual.\\\\n- **Multi-model generalizability**: Qwen3-32B primary + LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012).\\\\n- **MAVEN doesn't support hypothesis?**: Pairwise MiniLM does support it. Per-type centrality confound is methodological contribution, not failure.\\\\n- **Embedding sensitivity**: 2×3 ablation matrix is itself a contribution — characterizes when which proxy works.\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\\\\n|---------|------------------|------------|\\\\n| Only one model | LLaMA-3.3-70B replication | exp-012 |\\\\n| Cross-task replication | Pairwise: Few-NERD ✓ + MAVEN MiniLM ✓. Batch: FewRel ✓. Three tasks. | exp-002, exp-004, fewrel |\\\\n| No practical method | SDGD framework (-zero, -cal) | exp-008 to exp-011 |\\\\n| Confusability measure validity | Embedding ablation (D010) + pairwise vs per-type analysis | D010, exp-015 |\\\\n| Effect size small | Pairwise r=0.12-0.16 + FewRel Δ=21-50pp + confusion ratio 10-28x | multiple |\\\\n| MAVEN per-type opposite | Centrality confound analysis; pairwise still works | MAVEN MiniLM |\\\\n| Embedding choice matters? | 2×3 ablation table; SDGD-cal bypasses embedding entirely | D010 |\\\\n| Why not per-type splitting? | SDGD vs per-type vs random ablation | exp-013, exp-014 |\\\\n| Latency overhead | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 10641, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01McSQGkja8PgipZqGC6MdqF","session_id":"08464124-1424-4e01-848b-117232553f88","uuid":"6e60397c-1999-48e1-8986-2adff582bd94","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:38:38.674Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JdiyBww84Qcnu3AVnSBKeQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"SDGD-cal: empirical confusion-based type decomposition.\n2\t\n3\tReplaces embedding cosine similarity (SDGD-zero) with an empirical confusion\n4\tmatrix built from calibration samples. Addresses proxy failure on same-domain\n5\tschemas (e.g. MAVEN 168 event types) where embedding compression makes\n6\tcosine similarity unreliable.\n7\t\n8\tPipeline: run_calibration() -> build_confusion_matrix() -> confusion_to_similarity()\n9\t          -> (reuse SDGD-zero graph coloring infrastructure)\n10\t\"\"\"\n11\timport json\n12\timport logging\n13\tfrom collections import defaultdict\n14\tfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n15\t\n16\timport numpy as np\n17\t\n18\tlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n19\t\n20\t\n21\tdef build_confusion_matrix(\n22\t    predictions: List[List[Dict]],\n23\t    gold_labels: List[List[Dict]],\n24\t    type_list: List[str],\n25\t    task: str = \"ner\",\n26\t) -> np.ndarray:\n27\t    \"\"\"Build empirical confusion matrix from calibration predictions vs gold.\n28\t\n29\t    For NER/Event: match gold entities to predictions by text overlap.\n30\t    For RE: direct comparison of gold relation_type vs predicted relation\n31\t    (each sample has exactly one relation).\n32\t\n33\t    Args:\n34\t        predictions: per-sample predicted items\n35\t        gold_labels: per-sample gold items\n36\t        type_list: ordered list of type names\n37\t        task: 'ner', 'event', or 're'\n38\t\n39\t    Returns:\n40\t        confusion_matrix: (n_types, n_types) — C[i][j] = count of gold type i predicted as type j\n41\t    \"\"\"\n42\t    n = len(type_list)\n43\t    type_to_idx = {t: i for i, t in enumerate(type_list)}\n44\t    C = np.zeros((n, n), dtype=np.float64)\n45\t\n46\t    for sample_preds, sample_golds in zip(predictions, gold_labels):\n47\t        if task == \"re\":\n48\t            gold_rel = sample_golds[0].get(\"relation_type\", \"\") if sample_golds else \"\"\n49\t            pred_rel = sample_preds[0].get(\"relation\", \"\") if sample_preds else \"\"\n50\t            if gold_rel in type_to_idx and pred_rel in type_to_idx:\n51\t                C[type_to_idx[gold_rel]][type_to_idx[pred_rel]] += 1\n52\t            continue\n53\t\n54\t        for gold_ent in sample_golds:\n55\t            gold_type = gold_ent.get(\"type\", \"\")\n56\t            if gold_type not in type_to_idx:\n57\t                continue\n58\t            gold_text = gold_ent.get(\"text\", \"\").strip().lower()\n59\t            if not gold_text:\n60\t                continue\n61\t\n62\t            best_pred = _find_best_overlap(gold_ent, sample_preds)\n63\t            if best_pred is None:\n64\t                continue\n65\t\n66\t            pred_type = best_pred.get(\"type\", \"\")\n67\t            if pred_type not in type_to_idx:\n68\t                continue\n69\t\n70\t            gi = type_to_idx[gold_type]\n71\t            pi = type_to_idx[pred_type]\n72\t            C[gi][pi] += 1\n73\t\n74\t    logger.info(\n75\t        \"Confusion matrix: %d types, %d total confusions (off-diagonal), %d correct (diagonal)\",\n76\t        n,\n77\t        int(C.sum() - np.trace(C)),\n78\t        int(np.trace(C)),\n79\t    )\n80\t    return C\n81\t\n82\t\n83\tdef _find_best_overlap(\n84\t    gold_entity: Dict, predictions: List[Dict]\n85\t) -> Optional[Dict]:\n86\t    \"\"\"Find predicted entity with maximum character overlap to gold entity.\"\"\"\n87\t    gold_text = gold_entity.get(\"text\", \"\").strip().lower()\n88\t    if not gold_text:\n89\t        return None\n90\t\n91\t    best = None\n92\t    best_overlap = 0\n93\t\n94\t    for pred in predictions:\n95\t        pred_text = pred.get(\"text\", \"\").strip().lower()\n96\t        if not pred_text:\n97\t            continue\n98\t        overlap = _char_overlap(gold_text, pred_text)\n99\t        if overlap > best_overlap:\n100\t            best_overlap = overlap\n101\t            best = pred\n102\t\n103\t    if best_overlap == 0:\n104\t        return None\n105\t    return best\n106\t\n107\t\n108\tdef _char_overlap(a: str, b: str) -> int:\n109\t    \"\"\"Character-level overlap: length of longest common substring.\"\"\"\n110\t    if len(a) > len(b):\n111\t        a, b = b, a\n112\t    if a in b:\n113\t        return len(a)\n114\t    if b in a:\n115\t        return len(b)\n116\t    # simple: check if one is substring of the other, else use set intersection as proxy\n117\t    overlap = len(set(a) & set(b))\n118\t    # weighted by min length to prefer exact/near matches\n119\t    return overlap if overlap > len(a) * 0.5 else 0\n120\t\n121\t\n122\tdef confusion_to_similarity(\n123\t    confusion_matrix: np.ndarray,\n124\t    normalize: str = \"symmetric\",\n125\t) -> np.ndarray:\n126\t    \"\"\"Convert confusion matrix to similarity matrix compatible with SDGD graph pipeline.\n127\t\n128\t    Args:\n129\t        confusion_matrix: (n, n) raw counts\n130\t        normalize: 'symmetric' or 'row'\n131\t            symmetric: sim[i][j] = (C[i][j] + C[j][i]) / (sum(C[i,:]) + sum(C[j,:]))\n132\t            row: sim[i][j] = C[i][j] / sum(C[i,:])\n133\t\n134\t    Returns:\n135\t        similarity_matrix: (n, n) in [0, 1], diagonal = 0\n136\t    \"\"\"\n137\t    C = confusion_matrix.astype(np.float64)\n138\t    n = C.shape[0]\n139\t    sim = np.zeros((n, n), dtype=np.float64)\n140\t\n141\t    if normalize == \"symmetric\":\n142\t        row_sums = C.sum(axis=1)\n143\t        for i in range(n):\n144\t            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n145\t                denom = row_sums[i] + row_sums[j]\n146\t                if denom > 0:\n147\t                    sim[i][j] = (C[i][j] + C[j][i]) / denom\n148\t                    sim[j][i] = sim[i][j]\n149\t    elif normalize == \"row\":\n150\t        row_sums = C.sum(axis=1)\n151\t        for i in range(n):\n152\t            if row_sums[i] > 0:\n153\t                sim[i] = C[i] / row_sums[i]\n154\t        # symmetrize for graph construction\n155\t        sim = (sim + sim.T) / 2.0\n156\t    else:\n157\t        raise ValueError(f\"Unknown normalize mode: {normalize}\")\n158\t\n159\t    np.fill_diagonal(sim, 0.0)\n160\t\n161\t    # rescale to [0, 1] based on off-diagonal max\n162\t    off_diag = sim[~np.eye(n, dtype=bool)]\n163\t    if off_diag.max() > 0:\n164\t        sim = sim / off_diag.max()\n165\t    np.fill_diagonal(sim, 0.0)\n166\t\n167\t    logger.info(\n168\t        \"Confusion -> similarity: normalize=%s, max=%.4f, mean=%.4f, nonzero=%d/%d\",\n169\t        normalize,\n170\t        sim.max(),\n171\t        sim[~np.eye(n, dtype=bool)].mean(),\n172\t        (sim > 0).sum() - n,  # exclude diagonal\n173\t        n * (n - 1),\n174\t    )\n175\t    return sim\n176\t\n177\t\n178\tdef run_calibration(\n179\t    data_samples: List[Dict],\n180\t    type_list: List[str],\n181\t    type_definitions: Dict[str, str],\n182\t    model_path: str,\n183\t    task: str = \"ner\",\n184\t    n_samples: int = 100,\n185\t    prompt_version: str = \"v2\",\n186\t    max_model_len: int = 4096,\n187\t    dataset: str = \"fewnerd\",\n188\t) -> Tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n189\t    \"\"\"Run single-pass calibration on a sample subset to collect empirical confusion.\n190\t\n191\t    This is SDGD-cal step 0: infer all types on a small calibration set,\n192\t    then build the confusion matrix. Requires GPU (vLLM).\n193\t\n194\t    Args:\n195\t        data_samples: calibration data (will use first n_samples)\n196\t        type_list: all type names\n197\t        type_definitions: type_name -> description\n198\t        model_path: vLLM model path\n199\t        task: 'ner', 'event', or 're'\n200\t        n_samples: number of calibration samples\n201\t        prompt_version: prompt version\n202\t        max_model_len: vLLM max_model_len\n203\t        dataset: dataset name\n204\t\n205\t    Returns:\n206\t        (confusion_matrix, similarity_matrix) as numpy arrays\n207\t    \"\"\"\n208\t    import sys\n209\t    from pathlib import Path\n210\t    sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))\n211\t\n212\t    cal_samples = data_samples[:n_samples]\n213\t    logger.info(\"Running calibration on %d samples with %d types\", len(cal_samples), len(type_list))\n214\t\n215\t    # single-pass extraction with all types\n216\t    if task == \"re\":\n217\t        from run_extraction import build_json_schema_re, build_prompt_v2_re\n218\t        schema_fn = build_json_schema_re\n219\t        prompt_fn = None\n220\t    elif task == \"event\":\n221\t        from run_extraction import build_json_schema_v2\n222\t        schema_fn = build_json_schema_v2\n223\t        if prompt_version == \"v2\":\n224\t            from run_extraction import build_prompt_v2_event\n225\t            prompt_fn = build_prompt_v2_event\n226\t        else:\n227\t            from run_extraction import build_prompt_v1_event\n228\t            prompt_fn = build_prompt_v1_event\n229\t    else:\n230\t        if prompt_version == \"v2\":\n231\t            from run_extraction import build_json_schema_v2, build_prompt_v2\n232\t            schema_fn = build_json_schema_v2\n233\t            prompt_fn = build_prompt_v2\n234\t        else:\n235\t            from run_extraction import build_json_schema_v1, build_prompt_v1\n236\t            schema_fn = build_json_schema_v1\n237\t            prompt_fn = build_prompt_v1\n238\t\n239\t    from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n240\t    from vllm.sampling_params import StructuredOutputsParams\n241\t\n242\t    llm = LLM(\n243\t        model=model_path,\n244\t        dtype=\"bfloat16\",\n245\t        gpu_memory_utilization=0.95,\n246\t        enforce_eager=True,\n247\t        max_model_len=max_model_len,\n248\t        trust_remote_code=True,\n249\t        disable_log_stats=True,\n250\t    )\n251\t\n252\t    batch_schema = schema_fn(type_list)\n253\t    structured_params = StructuredOutputsParams(json=batch_schema)\n254\t    sampling_params = SamplingParams(\n255\t        max_tokens=1024, temperature=0.0, structured_outputs=structured_params,\n256\t    )\n257\t\n258\t    if task == \"re\":\n259\t        prompts = [\n260\t            build_prompt_v2_re(s[\"text\"], s[\"head_entity\"], s[\"tail_entity\"],\n261\t                               type_definitions, type_list)\n262\t            for s in cal_samples\n263\t        ]\n264\t    else:\n265\t        prompts = [prompt_fn(s[\"text\"], type_definitions, type_list) for s in cal_samples]\n266\t\n267\t    outputs = llm.generate(prompts, sampling_params)\n268\t\n269\t    predictions = []\n270\t    for output in outputs:\n271\t        try:\n272\t            preds = json.loads(output.outputs[0].text)\n273\t        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n274\t            preds = {}\n275\t        if task == \"re\":\n276\t            rel = preds.get(\"relation\", \"\") if isinstance(preds, dict) else \"\"\n277\t            predictions.append([{\"relation\": rel}])\n278\t        else:\n279\t            if isinstance(preds, dict):\n280\t                preds = preds.get(\"entities\", [])\n281\t            predictions.append(preds if isinstance(preds, list) else [])\n282\t\n283\t    gold_labels = []\n284\t    for s in cal_samples:\n285\t        if task == \"re\":\n286\t            gold_labels.append([{\"relation_type\": s.get(\"relation_type\", \"\")}])\n287\t        else:\n288\t            gold_labels.append(s.get(\"entities\", []))\n289\t\n290\t    C = build_confusion_matrix(predictions, gold_labels, type_list, task=task)\n291\t    sim = confusion_to_similarity(C, normalize=\"symmetric\")\n292\t\n293\t    logger.info(\"Calibration complete: confusion shape=%s\", C.shape)\n294\t    return C, sim\n295\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QXRrq61ytHCNXeWfzB2Uc5","session_id":"08464124-1424-4e01-848b-117232553f88","uuid":"3d9b2bf3-7cc4-4dba-b922-3ee1331969c0","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:38:38.363Z"}
