{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01McSQGkja8PgipZqGC6MdqF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the R2 (Novelty and Related Work) perspective:\n\n**What works:**\n- The two-channel degradation model (count -> recall, confusability -> type confusion) is genuinely novel -- no prior work disentangles these factors.\n- Pairwise SCS as a validated cross-task signal is a real contribution.\n- The centrality confound analysis on homogeneous schemas is a useful methodological insight.\n- The error taxonomy showing monotonic BCS-to-type-substitution gradient is compelling mechanistic evidence.\n\n**Critical issues:**\n1. **Result integrity**: Many results in the paper (DocRED, factorial ANOVA, SDGD v2/cal, ablations) match \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" in `review_context.yaml`. The experiment registry shows SDGD v2 as PREPARING, exp-012 as RUNNING, and SDGD-zero as failed (F1=0.251). If projected numbers are presented as empirical results, this is fatal.\n2. **Related work grossly inadequate**: 6 references when the project's own `literature_map.yaml` identifies 25+ relevant papers. Missing CrossAgentIE, IEPile, GoLLIE, GLiNER, PARSE, anisotropy literature, and more.\n3. **Effect sizes modest**: r=0.12-0.16 explains ~2% of variance; the title overstates the predictive power.\n4. **No experimental comparison with LLM-PEE** (the most direct competitor) or any non-LLM zero-shot baseline.\n5. **Dataset inconsistency**: Paper uses DocRED but actual experiments used FewRel.\n\nThe report is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4180df9a3ab94418 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4180df9a3ab94418' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 71663\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 159918</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"08464124-1424-4e01-848b-117232553f88","uuid":"a7faa17a-9994-488e-a8b0-7e90727cd946","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:41:06.229Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate originality, positioning against prior work, significance of contributions, and clarity of presentation.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Pairwise Type Confusability Predicts Schema-Induced Degradation in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper studies why training-free (in-context) LLM information extraction degrades on complex schemas. It proposes a multi-channel degradation model with two orthogonal channels:\n- Channel 1 (Count → Recall): More types dilute attention, causing entity omission\n- Channel 2 (Confusability → Type Confusion): Semantically similar co-present types cause type substitution errors\n\nKey metric: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type label embeddings. Pairwise SCS correlates with empirical confusion rates (r ≥ 0.12, p < 10^-6) across NER (Few-NERD, 66 types), RE (DocRED, 96 relations), and ED (MAVEN, 168 event types), and across two LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B).\n\nThe paper operationalizes this into SDGD (Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition):\n- SDGD-zero (embedding cosine similarity → graph coloring → batched extraction with merge-dedup)\n- SDGD-cal (empirical confusion matrix → same pipeline)\n- SDGD v2 with merge-dedup resolves earlier precision collapse issues\n\n## Paper Structure (8 pages)\n\n**Section 1: Introduction** (1 page)\n- Frames training-free LLM IE as important for scarce-data scenarios\n- Positions against GPT-NER [1] and Xie et al. [2] who observe degradation but don't characterize mechanisms\n- Positions against UniNER [3] (per-type splitting, no principled criterion) and LLM-PEE [4] (similarity grouping, no empirical validation)\n- 3 contributions: multi-channel model, pairwise confusability signal, SDGD framework\n\n**Section 2: Related Work** (0.5 pages)\nThree paragraphs:\n- Training-Free LLM IE: GPT-NER [1], Lost in the Middle [5]\n- Schema Decomposition: UniNER [3], LLM-PEE [4]\n- Type Confusion in IE: label smoothing work [6] in supervised settings\n\nOnly 6 references total.\n\n**Section 3: Multi-Channel Degradation Model** (1.5 pages)\n- Count curves on DocRED and LLaMA\n- BCS-controlled comparison on DocRED\n- 2×2 factorial with ANOVA and error decomposition\n\n**Section 4: Pairwise Type Confusability** (1.5 pages)\n- SCS-confusion correlation across 3 tasks\n- Cross-model validation (Qwen3 vs LLaMA)\n- Error taxonomy by BCS bin\n- Centrality confound in per-type aggregation\n\n**Section 5: SDGD** (1.5 pages)\n- Method: confusability graph → graph coloring → merge-dedup\n- Few-NERD results with ablations\n- MAVEN results (168 types)\n\n**Section 6: Analysis** (0.5 pages)\n- SCS proxy validation\n- Cross-task scaling\n\n**Section 7: Conclusion + Limitations** (0.5 pages)\n\n## Key Prior Work Referenced\n1. GPT-NER (Wang et al., EMNLP 2023) - Named entity recognition via LLMs\n2. Xie et al. (arXiv 2310, 2023) - Empirical study of training-free entity recognition\n3. UniNER (Zhou et al., ICLR 2024) - Per-type splitting via distillation\n4. LLM-PEE (Zhang et al., arXiv 2403, 2024) - LLM-based partition and ensemble\n5. Lost in the Middle (Liu et al., TACL 2024) - Long context attention patterns\n6. Li et al. (NAACL 2022) - Label smoothing in supervised QA\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How original are each of the 3 contributions? Is the multi-channel model genuinely new or a repackaging of known phenomena?\n2. **Related Work Coverage**: Are important baselines and related approaches missing? Is the positioning fair?\n3. **Significance**: Would this change how practitioners design IE systems? Is the impact sufficient for a top venue?\n4. **Presentation Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are the claims well-scoped? Is the structure logical?\n5. **Scope and Generalizability**: How limited is the two-model, decoder-only setting? Does \"training-free\" limit applicability?\n6. **Missing Comparisons**: What prior work should have been compared against?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work / Comparisons\n[What should have been cited or compared]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a4180df9a3ab94418","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\nKey findings from the R2 (Novelty and Related Work) perspective:\n\n**What works:**\n- The two-channel degradation model (count -> recall, confusability -> type confusion) is genuinely novel -- no prior work disentangles these factors.\n- Pairwise SCS as a validated cross-task signal is a real contribution.\n- The centrality confound analysis on homogeneous schemas is a useful methodological insight.\n- The error taxonomy showing monotonic BCS-to-type-substitution gradient is compelling mechanistic evidence.\n\n**Critical issues:**\n1. **Result integrity**: Many results in the paper (DocRED, factorial ANOVA, SDGD v2/cal, ablations) match \"imagined_experiments\" with \"expected_results\" in `review_context.yaml`. The experiment registry shows SDGD v2 as PREPARING, exp-012 as RUNNING, and SDGD-zero as failed (F1=0.251). If projected numbers are presented as empirical results, this is fatal.\n2. **Related work grossly inadequate**: 6 references when the project's own `literature_map.yaml` identifies 25+ relevant papers. Missing CrossAgentIE, IEPile, GoLLIE, GLiNER, PARSE, anisotropy literature, and more.\n3. **Effect sizes modest**: r=0.12-0.16 explains ~2% of variance; the title overstates the predictive power.\n4. **No experimental comparison with LLM-PEE** (the most direct competitor) or any non-LLM zero-shot baseline.\n5. **Dataset inconsistency**: Paper uses DocRED but actual experiments used FewRel.\n\nThe report is saved at `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":159918,"totalTokens":71663,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6262,"cache_read_input_tokens":64911,"output_tokens":489,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6262},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":489,"cache_read_input_tokens":64911,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6262,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6262,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
